<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
        Microphone. This app never opens an audio device itself — the
        entitlement exists for CLI tools the user runs in the docked
        terminal (agent CLIs with voice input, etc.).

        macOS TCC does not evaluate the process making the request. It walks
        up to the RESPONSIBLE process — the top-level .app bundle — and
        checks that bundle's entitlements and usage description. So a mic
        request from a PTY grandchild is authorized here, on the main
        binary, and nowhere else. The child needs no entitlement of its own.

        Under Hardened Runtime this key is what makes the app eligible to
        prompt at all; without it tccd denies silently, no dialog appears,
        and the app never lists in Privacy & Security > Microphone. It is a
        Hardened Runtime entitlement, not an App Sandbox one — the sandbox
        spelling (`device.microphone`) is only needed alongside it when
        `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is present, which it is not here.

        Deliberately absent from `entitlements.mac.inherit.plist`: under the
        responsible-process model helpers do not need it, and helper
        over-entitlement has caused an AMFI launch failure here before. The
        allowlist assertion in `tests/unit/entitlements-helper-split.test.ts`
        fails on any helper key not in its required set, so adding it there
        stays a conscious decision. Paired with `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`
        under `mac.extendInfo` in `electron-builder.yml` — the entitlement
        authorizes the prompt, that string is what the prompt says.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
    <array>
        <string>applinks:openknowledge.ai</string>
        <string>applinks:www.openknowledge.ai</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
